1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Here's something NESA's marking feedback raises every year.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,040
In every HSC Chemistry and Biology report from

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,800
2020 to 2025, it points out answers that

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,760
missed what the question actually asked.

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,840
Often the student knew the science.

6
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,240
They just answered a nearby question.

7
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:20,480
So this video is about the step before

8
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,760
you write anything: reading the question properly, in

9
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,120
about thirty seconds.

10
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:28,413
Here's an HSC-style question.

11
00:00:28,894 --> 00:00:32,173
It gives an equilibrium: iron three plus ions

12
00:00:32,254 --> 00:00:35,213
join thiocyanate ions to make a red ion.

13
00:00:35,854 --> 00:00:38,413
A few crystals of iron three nitrate dissolve

14
00:00:38,494 --> 00:00:41,133
in the tube, the temperature stays the same,

15
00:00:41,174 --> 00:00:43,293
and the solution turns a deeper red.

16
00:00:43,934 --> 00:00:45,773
Then it asks you to explain the colour

17
00:00:45,854 --> 00:00:47,853
change, in terms of collision theory.

18
00:00:48,254 --> 00:00:48,973
Three marks.

19
00:00:49,534 --> 00:00:51,773
Before you answer, let's pull it apart.

20
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,280
Start with the verb.

21
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,280
It tells you what to do with what

22
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:57,080
you know.

23
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,000
Here it's explain.

24
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:02,680
NESA's glossary describes explain as showing cause and

25
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:05,800
effect: how or why something happens.

26
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:07,960
So a list of facts won't do.

27
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,440
Your answer has to show why the colour

28
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:11,160
changes.

29
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:14,280
NESA also says to read every key word

30
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,520
in the context of its question, and the

31
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:18,680
next video is all about this one.

32
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:21,340
Next, the marks.

33
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,900
Three marks tells you roughly how much the

34
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:27,580
markers expect: about three linked points, which usually

35
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:29,340
fits the lines you're given.

36
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:31,580
It also tells you when to stop.

37
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:34,300
Ten lines on everything you know about equilibrium

38
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:35,820
won't earn a fourth mark.

39
00:01:37,034 --> 00:01:39,553
Now the content words and the givens.

40
00:01:39,834 --> 00:01:42,633
Underline what the question is about: the iron

41
00:01:42,714 --> 00:01:45,113
three nitrate, and the change to a deeper

42
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:45,513
red.

43
00:01:46,154 --> 00:01:48,313
Givens are usually there for a reason.

44
00:01:48,714 --> 00:01:51,113
For each one, ask what it tells you,

45
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:53,153
then decide if your answer needs it.

46
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:56,153
Iron three nitrate dissolves to give iron three

47
00:01:56,314 --> 00:01:59,113
plus ions, so a reactant goes up.

48
00:01:59,434 --> 00:02:01,513
And the temperature stays the same, so the

49
00:02:01,594 --> 00:02:03,433
collisions are no more energetic.

50
00:02:03,834 --> 00:02:06,073
Your answer is about how often the ions

51
00:02:06,154 --> 00:02:06,633
meet.

52
00:02:07,834 --> 00:02:10,953
Last, the constraint: the phrase that tells you

53
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:12,193
how to answer.

54
00:02:12,474 --> 00:02:15,833
Here it's "in terms of collision theory".

55
00:02:15,914 --> 00:02:18,633
In terms of means that idea must carry

56
00:02:18,674 --> 00:02:21,673
your explanation, so you'll talk about collisions and

57
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:22,633
rates.

58
00:02:23,034 --> 00:02:25,033
Two other phrases turn up a lot.

59
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:27,993
With reference to something means you name it

60
00:02:28,074 --> 00:02:29,633
and actually use it.

61
00:02:29,994 --> 00:02:32,713
Using the information means you take details from

62
00:02:32,754 --> 00:02:35,353
the stimulus, like numbers in a table, and

63
00:02:35,514 --> 00:02:36,633
say what they show.

64
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:40,833
So let's build the answer, in collision theory.

65
00:02:41,114 --> 00:02:43,833
If you take Biology, watch how each line

66
00:02:43,914 --> 00:02:45,233
uses the constraint.

67
00:02:45,514 --> 00:02:48,713
Adding iron three nitrate raises the concentration of

68
00:02:48,794 --> 00:02:51,513
iron three plus ions, so they collide with

69
00:02:51,594 --> 00:02:53,913
thiocyanate ions more often.

70
00:02:54,034 --> 00:02:57,033
That means more joining each second: the forward

71
00:02:57,194 --> 00:02:58,633
rate increases.

72
00:02:58,714 --> 00:03:00,713
That's the first point a marker can tick.

73
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:04,413
Now the link that makes it an explanation.

74
00:03:04,734 --> 00:03:07,693
The reverse reaction is the red ion splitting,

75
00:03:07,774 --> 00:03:10,093
which depends only on how many red ions

76
00:03:10,174 --> 00:03:10,573
there are.

77
00:03:11,214 --> 00:03:13,693
No extra red ions have formed yet, so

78
00:03:13,734 --> 00:03:15,853
the reverse rate hasn't changed.

79
00:03:16,174 --> 00:03:19,053
Forward is now faster than reverse, so red

80
00:03:19,134 --> 00:03:21,413
ions form faster than they split.

81
00:03:21,614 --> 00:03:22,413
That's point two.

82
00:03:22,894 --> 00:03:25,293
As red ions build up, the reverse rate

83
00:03:25,374 --> 00:03:28,173
rises and the forward rate falls, until the

84
00:03:28,294 --> 00:03:30,813
rates are equal again, with more red ion.

85
00:03:31,294 --> 00:03:32,893
That's why it's a deeper red.

86
00:03:33,534 --> 00:03:36,493
Three linked points, all in collision theory.

87
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:39,460
Now compare this answer.

88
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:43,300
"Adding iron three plus ions increases a reactant,

89
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:46,740
so by Le Chatelier's principle the equilibrium shifts

90
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:48,820
right to use some of it up, and

91
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:50,500
more of the red ion forms."

92
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:51,780
Is it wrong?

93
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:53,860
No, it's good chemistry.

94
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:56,980
But it never mentions collisions or rates.

95
00:03:57,380 --> 00:04:00,580
It answers a nearby question: explain using Le

96
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:01,700
Chatelier's principle.

97
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:04,740
In our marking guide it earns one mark,

98
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,060
for linking the shift to the deeper red.

99
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:11,146
So is it always one mark, one idea?

100
00:04:11,227 --> 00:04:13,466
As a rule of thumb, yes.

101
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:17,306
A three-mark explain usually needs about three points.

102
00:04:17,547 --> 00:04:18,906
But it has limits.

103
00:04:19,387 --> 00:04:21,306
The points have to be linked, not just

104
00:04:21,387 --> 00:04:21,946
listed.

105
00:04:22,587 --> 00:04:24,946
Some marks need a specific thing, like a

106
00:04:24,987 --> 00:04:27,546
balanced equation or a value from the data.

107
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:30,746
And longer answers are judged as a whole,

108
00:04:30,827 --> 00:04:33,306
so seven facts don't add up to seven

109
00:04:33,387 --> 00:04:34,026
marks.

110
00:04:35,234 --> 00:04:36,193
Here's the routine.

111
00:04:36,674 --> 00:04:38,953
Ask four questions, in order.

112
00:04:39,234 --> 00:04:40,073
Do what?

113
00:04:40,274 --> 00:04:41,233
That's the verb.

114
00:04:41,474 --> 00:04:42,273
How much?

115
00:04:42,434 --> 00:04:43,513
That's the marks.

116
00:04:43,794 --> 00:04:44,793
About what?

117
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:47,233
Those are the content words and givens.

118
00:04:47,554 --> 00:04:48,433
Using what?

119
00:04:48,594 --> 00:04:49,793
That's the constraint.

120
00:04:50,114 --> 00:04:51,833
It takes about thirty seconds.

121
00:04:52,034 --> 00:04:54,513
Then, when you've finished writing, go back and

122
00:04:54,594 --> 00:04:56,513
tick each part of the question your answer

123
00:04:56,754 --> 00:04:57,153
covers.

124
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:00,366
Your turn, with two questions.

125
00:05:00,847 --> 00:05:03,006
Do the one from your subject, or both.

126
00:05:03,647 --> 00:05:05,326
First, Biology.

127
00:05:05,647 --> 00:05:07,646
Roan cattle have a mix of red and

128
00:05:07,727 --> 00:05:08,686
white hairs.

129
00:05:09,247 --> 00:05:11,326
Roan cattle were crossed with each other, and

130
00:05:11,407 --> 00:05:15,086
the table shows their calves: 9 red, 21

131
00:05:15,167 --> 00:05:16,686
roan and 10 white.

132
00:05:17,407 --> 00:05:20,766
Using the information provided, explain how these results

133
00:05:20,887 --> 00:05:23,246
show that roan cattle are heterozygous.

134
00:05:23,807 --> 00:05:24,726
Three marks.

135
00:05:24,927 --> 00:05:26,606
Run the routine, then answer.

136
00:05:27,087 --> 00:05:28,366
Pause here to answer.

137
00:05:38,767 --> 00:05:41,806
The verb is explain, for three marks, and

138
00:05:41,887 --> 00:05:44,366
the constraint says use the data.

139
00:05:44,527 --> 00:05:47,566
So: roan parents had red calves and white

140
00:05:47,647 --> 00:05:52,366
calves, so each parent carries both alleles: heterozygous.

141
00:05:52,567 --> 00:05:55,566
Nine, twenty-one and ten is close to one

142
00:05:55,647 --> 00:05:58,366
to two to one, the ratio two heterozygous

143
00:05:58,447 --> 00:05:59,646
parents give.

144
00:05:59,727 --> 00:06:02,526
And roan calves are about half, the heterozygous

145
00:06:02,607 --> 00:06:03,646
share.

146
00:06:03,687 --> 00:06:07,166
The habit to drop: writing "codominance means both

147
00:06:07,287 --> 00:06:10,046
alleles show" and never using the numbers.

148
00:06:11,267 --> 00:06:12,586
Now Chemistry.

149
00:06:12,867 --> 00:06:17,506
Colourless dinitrogen tetroxide forms brown nitrogen dioxide in

150
00:06:17,547 --> 00:06:20,306
a sealed flask, and the forward reaction is

151
00:06:20,467 --> 00:06:21,346
endothermic.

152
00:06:21,907 --> 00:06:24,066
The flask is placed in ice water.

153
00:06:24,387 --> 00:06:27,346
With reference to Le Chatelier's principle, predict and

154
00:06:27,427 --> 00:06:29,106
explain the change in colour.

155
00:06:29,587 --> 00:06:30,546
Three marks.

156
00:06:30,787 --> 00:06:32,226
Pause here to answer.

157
00:06:42,634 --> 00:06:44,873
There are two verbs, so there are two

158
00:06:45,034 --> 00:06:45,753
jobs.

159
00:06:45,994 --> 00:06:48,753
Predict: the gas turns paler.

160
00:06:49,034 --> 00:06:52,073
Explain, and this time the constraint asks for

161
00:06:52,154 --> 00:06:54,833
Le Chatelier's principle, so name it.

162
00:06:55,114 --> 00:06:57,753
Cooling is the change, and the system partly

163
00:06:57,834 --> 00:07:00,393
counteracts it by favouring the reaction that gives

164
00:07:00,474 --> 00:07:01,273
out heat.

165
00:07:01,514 --> 00:07:05,113
That's the reverse reaction, so brown nitrogen dioxide

166
00:07:05,274 --> 00:07:08,353
turns into colourless dinitrogen tetroxide.

167
00:07:08,634 --> 00:07:11,913
The habit to drop: writing "it goes paler"

168
00:07:11,994 --> 00:07:12,833
and stopping.

169
00:07:13,034 --> 00:07:15,113
That's only one of the two jobs.

170
00:07:16,334 --> 00:07:19,613
So before you write anything: do what, how

171
00:07:19,774 --> 00:07:22,173
much, about what, using what.

172
00:07:22,654 --> 00:07:25,613
Answer exactly that question, not the one next

173
00:07:25,654 --> 00:07:26,373
to it.

174
00:07:26,814 --> 00:07:29,133
Next: the verb that turns up more than

175
00:07:29,294 --> 00:07:32,893
any other in HSC science, explain, and how

176
00:07:32,974 --> 00:07:35,533
it differs from identify and describe.

177
00:07:36,734 --> 00:07:38,173
Here are the key notes.

178
00:07:38,414 --> 00:07:40,333
Pause the video if you need more time

179
00:07:40,414 --> 00:07:41,213
to copy them.
